  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final detailed tutorial. Need use complete sentences. Avoid repeating. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, concrete examples, tradeoffs, pitfalls. Technical. Need likely mention stages: client connection, protocol, parsing, analysis, semantic checks, view expansion, planning/optimization, cost model, access paths, index selection, join strategies, execution engine, iterators/Volcano, MVCC transactions, locks, isolation levels, WAL, commit, result fetch, materialization/cursors. Include examples: schema, SQL query, EXPLAIN. Tradeoffs: indexes vs write overhead, join algorithms hash/nested loop/merge, stats, cardinality misestimates, temp tables, sorting, spilling. Pitfalls: missing indexes, OR causing index merge, select *, implicit casts, functions on indexed column, N+1, large IN, fan-out, deadlocks, long transactions, blocking, autocommit, high concurrency.\n\nNeed ensure complete sentences. Avoid repeating? We can be thorough but not redundant. Structure: title, overview, example schema, execution stages. Maybe code examples. Use headings.\n\nNeed no repeated content. Let's draft.\n\nPotential content:\n1. What happens when SQL arrives.\n2. Parsing and analysis: tokenizer/parser builds AST, semantic analysis, name resolution, type checking, privilege, view expansion, parameter binding.\n3. Planning/optimization: logical plan, rewrite rules, stats, cost estimation, choose access paths, join order, index selection. Explain optimizer: heuristic vs cost-based; selectivity; cardinality; EXPLAIN ANALYZE.\n4. Indexes and access paths: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, bitmap; indexes store rows/keys; range, lookup; covering; index-only scans; tradeoffs. Example: WHERE customer_id=?; composite index. Pitfalls: functions, leading wildcard, implicit cast, low cardinality, over-indexing.\n5. Execution: Volcano pipeline; operators: scan, filter, project, aggregate, sort, join; materialization; iterator model; example plan; row at a time; vectorization; parallelism.\n6. Joins: nested",
